// QR Code generation, bound to Nayuki's qrcodegen.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because qrcodegen depends on nothing a package manager could
// fetch -- the implementation includes `<assert.h>`, `<limits.h>`, `<stdlib.h>` and `<string.h>` and
// that is the whole list. Both files are carried in this package, because qrcodegen is not a library
// anybody has installed.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package:
//
//     dependencies {
//       qrcodegen { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/qrcodegen", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.0` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "qrcodegen"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

// **There is deliberately no `requires` block, and unlike qoi's that took no work at all.**
//
// A capability clause is a claim about what a library *does*, and what qrcodegen does is arithmetic
// on buffers it was handed. It opens no file, reads no clock, and -- the part that matters -- calls
// no allocator: every entry point takes the storage it will write into. There is no `malloc`,
// `calloc` or `free` anywhere in the 1027 lines, which is why this package can be built for a
// freestanding target without redirecting anything.
//
// **What it does use from the C library is `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `strlen` and `abs`**, plus
// `assert`. A freestanding toolchain supplies the first four whether or not it has a C library --
// clang emits calls to them from ordinary array code -- and `NDEBUG` compiles the assertions out.
// `requires {}` is a claim about what this package asks of *sysl*, and that claim is exact.
//
// **What is NOT here is a `@link` directive**, and the absence is the point of vendoring: the
// implementation is compiled from the `.c` carried in this package, so there is no external library
// for the linker to be given (`15 §8`).
